<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#concentration-camp">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Concentration camp</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A concentration camp is a place where large numbers of civilians or prisoners are detained, usually without trial, in harsh conditions. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> of 1899–1902, the British army interned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> women and children and black Africans in camps where many thousands died of disease. The camps damaged the international reputation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/concentration-camp.jpg" alt="Concentration camp" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Army Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boercamp1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…than a fraction of that figure, and the deaths of thousands of Boer and African civilians in British <b>concentration camps</b> damaged Britain's international reputation; and the two World Wars showed that industrial total war was beyond the sustainable capacity of an island of some 45 million people ruling…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Terror was organised by the SS under Heinrich Himmler, which absorbed the police, ran the Gestapo and the <b>concentration camps</b> from Dachau, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the Reich Security Main Office under Reinhard Heydrich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Steyn and de Wet refused to accept defeat and waged a guerrilla war for two more years, during which British forces burned farms across the Free State and moved much of its population into <b>concentration camps</b>, where mortality was very high.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The state answered with protected villages, which the guerrillas compared to <b>concentration camps</b>, with raids into neighbouring states such as Operation Dingo in 1977, and with chemical and biological agents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The war of 1899–1902, in which the British Empire deployed some 450,000 troops against two small republics, was Britain’s most expensive conflict between the Napoleonic Wars and the First World War and provoked fierce criticism at home, above all over the <b>concentration camps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>About twenty Bauhauslers are known to have been killed in prisons or <b>concentration camps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · People</a></li></ul></section>
